<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="exam1">
    <title>Exam 1 - MATH-262-02</title>

    <p>
        <alert>Instructions:</alert> You have 110 minutes to take this exam. Please answer each question in the space provided. Answers without justification may <alert> earn no credit </alert>. You are permitted your signed prep work journal and a calculator on this exam. No other notes or electronic devices of any kind are allowed on this exam. Failure to comply may result in earning a 0 on any portion of this exam and/or being reported to the university. 
    </p>

    <p>
        <alert>Note:</alert> Unless otherwise stated, you may give implicit solutions. 
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                [20 points] Classify the differential equation <md> x^2 y'' + xy' + x^2 y = y + \ln(x) </md> according to its type, order, linearity, and homogeneity. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                [10 points] Verify that <md> r(\theta) = \frac{1}{\frac{1}{2} - \ln(\theta) } </md> is a solution to the equation <md> \deriv{r}{\theta} = \frac{r^2}{\theta} </md> and find its largest interval of existence.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                [18 points] Determine whether the differential equation <md> 6y^2x \, dx - dy = 0 </md> is separable, exact, and/or linear. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                [14 points] Solve <md> 2\deriv{u}{t} - u = 4 \sin(3t). </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                <m> \int e^{ax} \sin(bx) \, dx = \frac{e^{ax}}{a^2 + b^2} (a \sin(bx) - b \cos(bx)) + C</m>.
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                [16 points] Solve <md> \dfrac{2ty}{t^2 + 1} - 2t - (2 - \ln(t^2 + 1)) y'(t) = 0.</md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                [18 points] Use the substitution <md> u = 4x - y </md> to solve the IVP <md> y' = e^{4x - y} </md> While you may give your solution implicitly, it should not include <m>u</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                <m> \int \frac{1}{a - e^x} \, dx = \frac{1}{a} (x - \ln(a(a-e^x))) + C.</m>
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                [32 points] A large tank catches runoff from a chemical process. Suppose the tank initially contains 800 gallons of polluted water with 2 oz of pollutants. Polluted water runoff begins to flow into the tank at 3 gal/min. The runoff contains 5 oz of pollutants per gallon. The solution is piped out of the tank to a water treatment plant at 3 gal/min. You may assume the contents of the tank remain well-mixed. 
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a first-order IVP that models the amount (in oz) of pollutants <m>p</m> after <m>t</m> minutes.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the equation you defined in part (a) using any method. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How long does it take for the tank to accumulate 4 oz of pollutants? 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that regulation required the chemical process to be slowed if the tank ever contains more than 4oz of pollutants. When this happens, the runoff slows to 2 gal/min. How would this affect the ODE model you wrote in part (a)? The algebraic model you found in part (b)?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    
</worksheet>
